<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Attica is the peninsula of east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> that formed the territory of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> to the north and by the sea on its other sides, it includes the plain of Athens, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurion</a> and the marble quarries of Mount Pentelikon. Its communities were united under Athens by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and it is today an administrative region of Greece.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attica.jpg" alt="Attica" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Hills_of_Hymettus_10_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Attica, or the Attic Peninsula, is a triangular peninsula projecting into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>. It is divided from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> to the north by the Cithaeron and Parnes ranges and borders Megaris to the west. Mountains divide it into the plains of Pedias, Mesogeia and the Thriasian Plain, and four of them, Aigaleo, Parnitha, Penteli and Hymettus, enclose the hilly plain on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> urban area now spreads. The mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurion</a> lie at the southern tip, near Cape Sounion. According to Pausanias the place was originally named Actaea and was renamed for Atthis, daughter of King Cranaus of Athens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traditions of the classical period held that during the Greek Dark Ages Attica became a refuge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a>, and that many later left to found the twelve cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. In the Mycenaean period the inhabitants lived in autonomous agricultural societies. Tradition said that twelve small communities were united under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>; many modern historians think they were incorporated progressively into an Athenian state, though they differ on when this happened.</p><p>Aristocratic families lived independently until the tyranny of Peisistratos and the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> in 508/7 BCE, which divided Attica into about a hundred demes in three zones, the city, the coast and the inland, grouped into ten tribes. During the Peloponnesian war Lacedaemonian forces repeatedly raided Attica and fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decelea">Decelea</a>. The worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and Cora at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> lasted from the Mycenaean period to the late years of antiquity.</p><p>Attica later came under Roman, Byzantine, Venetian and Ottoman rule, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries; the Heruli raided it in 267 CE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> under Alaric invaded in 396. Its peasants were the first to revolt in the Greek War of Independence, in April 1821, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> became the Greek capital in 1834.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Historical Attica encompasses the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> metropolitan area. Refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and later the population exchanges under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a>, brought a surge of settlers. The modern administrative region is larger than the historical one: it includes Megaris as West Attica, the Saronic Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Cythera</a> and the municipality of Troizinia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Attica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries the villages of <b>Attica</b>, an area of some 2,500 square kilometres, were drawn into a single state centred on Athens, a process the Athenians credited to the hero Theseus and called the synoikismos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Boeotia, the fertile plain of central Greece between <b>Attica</b> and Phocis, held a dozen or more independent cities, of which Thebes was the largest, followed by Orchomenus, Thespiae, Tanagra and Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Peloponnesian War of 431–404 BCE Sparta invaded <b>Attica</b> repeatedly, but the surrender of some 120 Spartiates on Sphacteria in 425 BCE shocked the Greek world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thebes stands in a plain of Boeotia between Lake Yliki, the ancient Hylica, to the north and the Cithaeron mountains, which divide Boeotia from <b>Attica</b>, to the south, about 50 km northwest of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 4th century BCE <b>Attica</b> held perhaps 30,000 adult male citizens beside some 10,000 resident foreigners and many thousands of slaves, and women had no political rights.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Only citizens could vote, hold office and own land in <b>Attica</b>, and after Pericles’s citizenship law of 451 BCE a citizen had to be born of an Athenian father and an Athenian mother.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
